    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**Secure service workers** is an enterprise security-architecture topic. Explain trust boundaries, ownership, governance, identity, secrets, observability, and how the organization continuously enforces the control.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the protected asset, attacker capability, trust boundary, secure default, enforcement point, and recovery path before writing code.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core policy or state check so you can explain the security reasoning line by line during the interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Control {\n  name: string;\n  owner: string;\n  enabled: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction enabledControls(\n  controls: readonly Control[],\n): string[] {\n  const result: string[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < controls.length; i += 1) {\n    if (controls[i].enabled) {\n      result.push(controls[i].name);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst controls = enabledControls([\n  { name: \"mfa\", owner: \"identity\", enabled: true },\n  { name: \"audit\", owner: \"platform\", enabled: true },\n]);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or browser APIs when they make the same control clearer without hiding the security boundary.\n\n```ts\nconst controls = [\n  { name: \"mfa\", owner: \"identity\", enabled: true },\n  { name: \"audit\", owner: \"platform\", enabled: true },\n];\n\nconst enabled = controls\n  .filter((control) => control.enabled)\n  .map((control) => control.name);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse Secure service workers as an organization-wide control with explicit ownership, evidence, monitoring, exceptions, and review cadence.\n\nStep 6 — Validation:\nTest the intended path, denied path, malformed input, missing/expired state, misconfiguration, and recovery/rollback behavior where applicable.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what is trusted, what is untrusted, where final enforcement occurs, why the default is safe, and how monitoring or testing prevents recurrence.",
